WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.110
Now, let's talk about Claude's long-term

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.990
memory, which is contained by this file

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.430
called memory.md. Now, if you recall, by

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.710
default, Claude Code has two levels of

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.030
memory broadly. One is the Claude.md,

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.110
which is its employee handbook. It reads

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.430
the whole thing in one go every session.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.070
And the other thing that it reads is

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.710
this memory.md. And in its essence, a

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.110
memory.md is actually just a short list.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.470
It is a reference file that points to

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.310
the different artifacts or memory

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.830
artifacts that you have accumulated as

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.510
you converse with Claude Code. And so,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.990
if you look at that example of a

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.590
memory.md, you can see here that it is

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.310
actually not that long. It's only

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.350
something like 75 lines in here. And

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.470
what it is is an index. So, instead of

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.750
loading all of the memory artifacts in

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.990
this one long file, what it is is

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.870
basically points to different markdown

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.630
files that contain different memories

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.310
that have been stored as you converse

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.910
with your agent. And if we look at this

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.110
memory feedback.md to give an example,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.830
you can see here that it is another

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.390
pointer to different markdown files on

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.550
feedback that I have given Claude Code

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.070
as we work together. To give one

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.710
example, I have this feedback that I

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.830
want all my content examples when I'm

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.070
co-creating it with Claude Code to be as

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.670
non-technical as possible so that they

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.510
are very accessible to people. And if I

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.310
look at that markdown file, this

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.470
feedback audience non-technical.md, what

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.870
that basically is is another text file

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.230
that has a name, it has a description,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.910
and it also has a body which Claude Code

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.750
reads whenever it deems it necessary.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.550
So, in this case, it will read it when

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.510
we are working on a particular content

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.310
piece together because it will identify

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.870
that this particular memory artifact is

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.910
relevant to the task at hand. And so, if

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.830
you think about it, memory.md, all it

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.110
really is is it's an index. It gives

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.110
Claude Code the ability to look through

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.150
the file cabinet and actually find

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.710
relevant artifacts, relevant

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.270
conversations from your past so that it

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.800
can assist you better.
